vours first:  burning sensation, deformity of the arm or hand, difficulty moving the shoulder, arm, hand, or fingers, diminished arm reflexes, electric shock-like sensation\n  Favours second: 2-3 toe syndactyly, abdominal obesity, abnormal cardiac septum morphology, abnormal cardiac ventricular function, abnormal cerebral white matter morphology\n\nBrachial Plexopathy vs Toxic Neuropathy:\n  Favours first:  burning sensation, deformity of the arm or hand, difficulty moving the shoulder, arm, hand, or fingers, diminished arm reflexes, electric shock-like sensation\n  Favours second: abnormal acetabulum morphology, abnormal aortic morphology, abnormal aortic valve morphology, abnormal cerebral white matter morphology, abnormal clavicle morphology\n\nRadial Neuropathy vs Diabetic Neuropathy:\n  Favours first:  inability to extend fingers and thumb, numbness or tingling in the posterior forearm, pain in the lateral forearm, sensory deficits in the dorsoradial hand, weakness in grip and pinch\n  Favours second: bell's palsy, bladder incontinence, burning pain in feet and legs, constipation, diarrhea\n\nRadial Neuropathy vs Toxic Neuropathy:\n  Favours first:  2-3 toe syndactyly, abdominal obesity, abnormal cardiac septum morphology, abnormal cardiac ventricular function, abnormal cortical gyration\n  Favours second: abnormal acetabulum morphology, abnormal aortic morphology, abnormal aortic valve morphology, abnormal clavicle morphology, abnormal corpus callosum morphology\n\nDiabetic Neuropathy vs Toxic Neuropathy:\n  Favours first:  2-3 toe syndactyly, abdominal obesity, abnormal cardiac septum morphology, abnormal cardiac ventricular function, abnormal cortical gyration\n  Favours second: abnormal acetabulum morphology, abnormal aortic morphology, abnormal aortic valve morphology, abnormal clavicle morphology, abnormal corpus callosum morphology\n\nRelated to Neurovascular Compression Syndrome: carpal tunnel syndrome, meralgia paresthetica, peripheral neuropathy\n\nRe